Z||000004||maryjane||09-29-2001||02:31 AM||||A good article! However,all that air power won't do much good against the mountains, caves and boulders in Afganistan. They absorb too much of the energy released from munitions and not much left to affect humans. Too many places to hide. BUT, there is a way. The Marine helicopter squadron I was with in Vietnam found a way to set entire mountains and islands afire. Way before 'apocolypse now', our squadron sign said "Napalm is nature's way" I have some pictures somewhere I'll dig out later. <A HREF="http://www.hmh-463-vietnam.com/reflections/yourstory.html#firststory" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hmh-463-vietnam.com/reflections/yourstory.html#firststory</A> <BR>Click on the items about 'barrel bombing' or 'memories of a thrash light'. What the stories don't tell is that we had tens of thousands of gallons of nape sitting on our flight line for several weeks waiting for clear weather. Glad Charlie didn't drop a mortar round or rocket on it.<BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/marine_corp_mounted_md_wht_1.gif">    <BR>no prisoners<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
